# 2026-03-23 — Today I Learned: structuredClone for deep cloning in JS/TS

structuredClone(obj) performs a deep, structured copy (handles Dates, RegExps, Maps, Sets, typed arrays, circular refs) unlike JSON serialization. It throws on functions, DOM nodes, and some host objects.

Example:
```js
const original = { d: new Date(), m: new Map([['k','v']]), arr: [1,2, {x:3}] };
const copy = structuredClone(original);
console.log(copy.d instanceof Date); // true
console.log(copy.m.get('k')); // "v"
copy.arr[2].x = 42;
console.log(original.arr[2].x); // 3 (unchanged)
```

Fallback (when structuredClone isn't available):
```js
function deepCloneFallback(obj) {
  if (typeof structuredClone === 'function') return structuredClone(obj);
  // best-effort fallback: MessageChannel-based clone (works in many environments)
  return new Promise((res) => {
    const {port1, port2} = new MessageChannel();
    port2.onmessage = e => res(e.data);
    port1.postMessage(obj);
  });
}
```

Caveats: structuredClone is synchronous; fallback above is async. Functions and certain platform objects cannot be cloned. Use it when you need reliable deep copies without serialization loss.